# Counter-example: revert the merge instead of resetting past it.
#
# `git revert -m 1` is the correct tool for a merge that has been PUBLISHED --
# it undoes the content with a new commit and leaves history alone. Here that
# is exactly the wrong property: main keeps the merge commit, keeps the
# unfinished branch in its ancestry, and gains a second commit explaining the
# first. Merging feature/search again later then does nothing at all, because
# git still considers it merged.

git log --oneline --graph --decorate
git revert --no-edit -m 1 HEAD
git log --oneline --graph --decorate
ls
